.TH DATAWIPE 1 "October 2026" "datawipe 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
datawipe \- sanitize a disk, verify the result and issue a certificate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datawipe
[\fBinteractive\fR] [\fB\-\-i\-understand\-this\-is\-destructive\fR] [\fB\-\-experimental\fR] [\fB\-\-state\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B datawipe probe
\fItarget\fR [\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIprofile.json\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B datawipe plan
\fItarget\fR [\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIprofile.json\fR] [\fB\-\-experimental\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B datawipe run
\fItarget\fR [\fB\-\-identity\fR \fIprofile.json\fR] [\fB\-\-resume\fR | \fB\-\-restart\fR] [\fB\-\-sign\-key\fR \fIkey.pem\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B datawipe verify
\fItarget\fR \fB\-\-cert\fR \fIcertificate.json\fR
.br
.B datawipe verify\-cert
\fIcertificate.json\fR|\fI.pdf\fR [\fB\-\-pubkey\fR \fIissuer.pub.pem\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR \fIsession.dwlog.jsonl\fR]
.br
.B datawipe keygen
\fIprefix\fR [\fB\-\-no\-passphrase\fR]
.br
.B datawipe ata\-unlock
\fI/dev/sdX\fR \fB\-\-i\-understand\-this\-is\-destructive \-\-experimental\fR [\fB\-\-password\fR \fIP\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B datawipe
erases storage media following NIST SP 800\-88 (Clear and Purge), reads back a
risk\-weighted sample of the medium to verify the result (ARVS), scores it with the
Sanitization Confidence Index (SCI), and writes a certificate whose session history is a
hash chain signed with the issuing organisation's Ed25519 key (TECC).
.PP
A \fItarget\fR is a disk image file, a loop device backed by an image the invoking user
owns, or a physical disk. Image files and loop devices stand in for a drive described by
\fB\-\-identity\fR; they are for trials and tests. Partitions are always refused.
.PP
With no command on a terminal, or with \fBinteractive\fR, a guided menu is shown: list
drives, inspect one, wipe, verify a certificate, create a signing key.
.SH SAFETY
\fBWiping destroys all data on the target and cannot be undone.\fR
A physical disk is written only when all of the following hold: the process runs as root;
the drive identified itself with its own commands; nothing uses it (mounts, swap, LVM, RAID,
device\-mapper); \fB\-\-i\-understand\-this\-is\-destructive\fR was given; and the operator,
at an interactive terminal, retyped the drive's serial number and then its device path.
The guided mode lists physical disks only when started with that flag.
.PP
\fBprobe\fR and \fBplan\fR are read\-only.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B probe
Detect and classify the target (model, serial, size, media type, hidden areas, firmware
erase support).
.TP
.B plan
Probe, then show the sanitization method that would be used, step by step. Writes nothing.
.TP
.B run
Execute the plan, verify it and write the certificate (JSON, PDF copy and session log).
.TP
.B verify
Re\-read a previously wiped medium against its certificate.
.TP
.B verify\-cert
Check a certificate's seal, signature and, with \fB\-\-log\fR, its full session history.
.TP
.B keygen
Create an organisation signing key pair: \fIprefix\fR.key.pem (private, passphrase
protected) and \fIprefix\fR.pub.pem (to publish).
.TP
.B ata\-unlock
Remove the ATA password left on a drive by an interrupted Secure Erase.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-i\-understand\-this\-is\-destructive
Required to write a physical disk, and for the guided mode to list physical disks.
.TP
.B \-\-experimental
Also use firmware commands that have not yet been validated on real hardware by this
project. Without it, plans fall back to validated methods and say what was left out.
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " dir"
Where journals, certificates and keys/ for physical disks are kept (default: the current
directory, or \fBDATAWIPE_STATE_DIR\fR). Prefer this option under sudo.
.TP
.BI \-\-sign\-key " key.pem"
Sign the certificate with this private key. Without a key the certificate is unsigned:
its integrity can be checked, its origin cannot.
.TP
.BR \-\-resume ", " \-\-restart
Continue an interrupted wipe from its journal, or discard the journal and begin again.
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " path"
Certificate path (for \fBrun\fR: where to write it; for \fBverify\fR: which to check against).
.TP
.BR \-\-sampler " arvs|uniform, " \-\-verify\-budget " \fIpercent\fR, " \-\-fixed\-budget ", " \-\-sci\-lambda " \fIn\fR, " \-\-rewipe\-attempts " \fIn\fR, " \-\-no\-verify
Verification settings. Run \fBdatawipe\fR with a wrong argument to print the full list.
.TP
.BR \-\-keep\-hidden ", " \-\-no\-defense\-in\-depth ", " \-\-direct ", " \-\-buffer\-size " \fIsize\fR, " \-\-sync\-every " \fIsize\fR, " \-\-journal " \fIpath\fR"
Execution settings.
.TP
.BR \-\-json ", " \-v
Machine\-readable output; verbose logging.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TS
l l.
0	completed
1	usage error
2	refused by a safety check
3	detection failed
4	not supported
5	error
6	a plan step failed (journal kept)
7	interrupted (resumable)
8	verification failed, or certificate check failed
10	hidden area (HPA/DCO) removed: run again to wipe the full capacity
.TE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DATAWIPE_STATE_DIR
Default for \fB\-\-state\-dir\fR.
.TP
.B DATAWIPE_SIGN_KEY
Default for \fB\-\-sign\-key\fR.
.TP
.B DATAWIPE_KEY_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase of the signing key, instead of a terminal prompt.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour in the guided mode.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/datawipe/profiles/
Simulated drive profiles for use with image files and loop devices.
.TP
.I /usr/share/datawipe/hardware_test.sh
Spare\-drive test that validates firmware commands on real hardware.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/datawipe/README.md
Full documentation, including what has and has not been validated on real drives.
.SH EXAMPLES
Try it on a scratch image:
.PP
.nf
.RS
truncate \-s 64M disk.img
datawipe run disk.img \-\-identity /usr/share/datawipe/profiles/hdd_basic.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Wipe a real disk through the guided mode:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo datawipe interactive \-\-i\-understand\-this\-is\-destructive \-\-state\-dir ~/datawipe\-state
.RE
.fi
